(() => {
  const lesson = (module, title, heading, intro, bullets, code, tip, question, options, answer) => ({
    module, title, duration: "12 min", heading, intro, bullets, code, tip, question, options, answer
  });

  window.learningPaths["software-testing"] = {
    title: "Manual & Automation Testing",
    mark: "QA",
    category: "SOFTWARE QUALITY",
    accent: "#187c70",
    soft: "#e8f5f1",
    summary: "Learn software quality from SDLC and STLC through test design, realistic test data, defect tracking, automation, and continuous delivery.",
    references: [
      { label: "ISTQB Foundation Level overview", url: "https://www.istqb.org/certifications/certified-tester-foundation-level" },
      { label: "Playwright testing documentation", url: "https://playwright.dev/docs/intro" }
    ],
    lessons: [
      lesson("Foundations & lifecycle", "Software quality and the tester's role", "Build quality into the product, not just the final test", "Software testing evaluates a product and its work products to discover defects, reduce risk, and provide evidence for decisions. Testing cannot prove the absence of every defect. Quality is shared by the whole team; testers add an independent, user-focused perspective.", ["Distinguish error (a human mistake), defect (a flaw in an artifact), and failure (incorrect behavior during execution).", "Use verification to ask whether work meets its specified requirements and validation to ask whether it solves the user's need.", "Report evidence and risk honestly. A passed test describes tested conditions, not every possible use."], "Requirement: a locked account cannot sign in.\nTest evidence: after five invalid attempts, the account was still able to sign in.\n\nObserved behavior is evidence of a defect;\nrepeat the steps and record the account state.", "Testing provides information about quality and risk; it is not a guarantee of zero defects.", "What can a passing test establish?", ["The product has no defects", "The tested conditions behaved as expected", "Every user workflow is correct"], 1),
      lesson("Foundations & lifecycle", "Manual testing and human judgment", "Use observation and investigation where people add value", "Manual testing is a person executing or exploring tests without relying on a script to perform every action and evaluation. It is effective for exploratory investigation, usability, unclear requirements, and one-off checks. It still needs an objective, evidence, and repeatable notes when defects are reported.", ["Start with a goal, a test charter or cases, and a known build and environment.", "Observe the actual result rather than confirming only what you expected to see.", "Capture exact inputs, sequence, account state, timestamps, and evidence for repeatable failures."], "Charter: Explore password reset for a new user.\nObserve: delivery, link expiry, one-time use,\nerror messages, keyboard flow, and account state.\nRecord build, browser, data, steps, and result.", "Manual testing is not unstructured clicking: investigation should be purposeful and evidence-based.", "Which task most benefits from human exploratory judgment?", ["Checking the same stable calculation on 500 builds", "Exploring whether a confusing workflow makes sense to new users", "Verifying a fixed API schema every commit"], 1),
      lesson("Foundations & lifecycle", "Test automation and its limits", "Automate repeatable checks with valuable feedback", "Test automation uses software to set up, execute, compare, and report checks. It makes frequent and repeatable validation practical, but scripts have maintenance costs and can encode mistaken assumptions. Automate stable, high-value checks; use people for exploration and judgment.", ["Automate deterministic, repeatable, frequently run checks with clear expected outcomes.", "Include setup, assertions, cleanup, useful diagnostics, and ownership in the automation design.", "Review failures: a red test can indicate a product defect, a test defect, bad data, or an environment problem."], "Given a repeatable checkout flow:\n1. Prepare an isolated account and cart.\n2. Submit a valid order through the UI or API.\n3. Assert the order ID and persisted status.\n4. Clean up created data and report diagnostics.", "Automation improves speed and repeatability; it does not replace test design or all manual testing.", "When is automation most valuable?", ["When a check is repeatable, valuable, and expected frequently", "Whenever a test has no defined expected result", "Only when a tester is unavailable"], 0),
      lesson("Foundations & lifecycle", "SDLC: software development lifecycle", "Understand where testing fits across development", "The SDLC describes how software is planned, designed, built, tested, released, and maintained. Teams may use sequential, iterative, or continuous approaches. Testing works best when it begins with requirements and continues as the product changes, rather than being postponed to a final phase.", ["Common activities include discovery, requirements, design, implementation, verification, release, and maintenance.", "In iterative development, refine and test small increments with regular feedback.", "Shift-left means considering quality earlier; shift-right adds observation and validation of real production behavior."], "Idea → requirements → design → build → verify → release → operate\n\nIterative teams revisit these activities for each increment.\nProduction monitoring feeds new evidence into planning.", "SDLC labels vary by organization; focus on the actual work, handoffs, and feedback loops.", "What does shift-left testing emphasize?", ["Testing and quality activities earlier in development", "Testing only after production release", "Removing requirements review"], 0),
      lesson("Foundations & lifecycle", "STLC: software testing lifecycle", "Plan, design, execute, and close testing deliberately", "The STLC organizes testing work from requirement analysis through test planning, design, environment and data preparation, execution, defect handling, and closure. These activities can overlap and repeat. Entry and exit criteria make readiness and remaining risk visible.", ["Analyze testable requirements and identify ambiguity, dependencies, and risk.", "Plan scope, approach, resources, environments, schedule, and entry/exit criteria.", "Design cases, prepare data, execute and report; close with results, residual risk, and improvement actions."], "STLC work products:\nAnalysis: requirement questions and risks\nPlan: scope, approach, entry/exit criteria\nDesign: scenarios, cases, data\nExecution: results, defects, coverage\nClosure: summary, open risk, lessons learned", "Use the STLC as a practical checklist, not a rigid sequence that prevents useful iteration.", "Which artifact typically describes scope, approach, resources, and risks?", ["Test plan", "Defect retest result", "Production source code"], 0),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Test strategy, plan, and estimation", "Make scope, risks, and decisions explicit", "A test strategy describes the overall quality approach, while a test plan applies it to a project, release, or feature. A useful plan states scope and exclusions, test levels and types, responsibilities, environments, data, schedule, risks, entry/exit criteria, and reporting. Estimation should account for uncertainty and dependencies.", ["Separate organization-wide standards from release-specific test plans.", "Prioritize the highest-impact risks and identify what will not be tested.", "Estimate analysis, design, data setup, execution, automation, defect retest, and contingency."], "Plan outline:\nScope / exclusions\nRisks and priorities\nLevels, types, and techniques\nPeople, environments, and test data\nSchedule and dependencies\nEntry / exit criteria and reporting", "A plan should communicate assumptions and residual risk rather than promise impossible exhaustive coverage.", "What is the most useful purpose of explicit exclusions?", ["Hide incomplete work", "Make untested scope and its risk visible", "Guarantee that the release is safe"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Test levels and test types", "Choose the right target and quality characteristic", "Test levels describe scope: component/unit tests, integration tests, system tests, and acceptance tests. Test types describe purpose or quality characteristic: functional, performance, security, usability, compatibility, and more. A level can include multiple types.", ["Component testing checks a unit in isolation; integration checks interactions and contracts.", "System testing checks end-to-end behavior against system requirements; acceptance evaluates fitness for users or business.", "Select non-functional checks according to risks such as latency, accessibility, resilience, and security."], "Example: checkout\nUnit: discount calculation\nIntegration: order service ↔ payment sandbox\nSystem: complete purchase journey\nAcceptance: business owner validates refund policy\nPerformance: load and response-time targets", "Do not confuse test level (where/how much) with test type (what quality or behavior).", "Performance testing is best classified as a...", ["test level", "test type", "defect priority"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Functional and non-functional testing", "Validate behavior and qualities under stated conditions", "Functional testing checks what the system does against requirements, including valid behavior, rules, errors, and permissions. Non-functional testing checks how well it works, such as performance, accessibility, security, reliability, compatibility, and usability. Both need measurable acceptance criteria.", ["Derive functional checks from user workflows, business rules, interfaces, and error paths.", "Make non-functional targets observable: for example, response-time percentiles under a defined load.", "Test quality characteristics on representative devices, data, networks, and user roles."], "Functional: a valid coupon reduces the order total once.\nPerformance: p95 search latency stays below\n400 ms at 300 concurrent users.\nAccessibility: every checkout action is\nkeyboard reachable with visible focus.", "A vague quality target such as “fast” or “secure” needs clarification before it can be tested well.", "Which is a non-functional acceptance criterion?", ["An order receives a unique ID", "Search p95 stays under 400 ms at the agreed load", "A required field rejects empty input"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Black-box design: equivalence partitioning", "Reduce cases by grouping inputs with similar behavior", "Equivalence partitioning divides an input domain into groups expected to behave similarly. Select representative valid and invalid values from each partition. The technique reduces redundant cases but depends on correctly identifying partitions and rules.", ["List valid and invalid classes for each input and relevant combination.", "Choose at least one representative value per partition; add more when risk warrants.", "Include meaningful distinctions such as missing, malformed, unauthorized, and out-of-range data."], "Age rule: 18 through 65 inclusive\nPartitions:\n  invalid: age < 18\n  valid: 18 <= age <= 65\n  invalid: age > 65\nRepresentatives: 17, 30, 66", "Partition by expected behavior, not merely by whether values look alike.", "For an age rule of 18–65 inclusive, which set is a valid partition representative?", ["17", "42", "66"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Boundary value analysis", "Probe edges where off-by-one defects cluster", "Boundary value analysis tests values at and near the edges of an ordered range because defects often occur at transitions. For inclusive limits, test the minimum, just below it, just above it, maximum, just below it, and just above it. Combine with equivalence classes.", ["Confirm whether limits are inclusive or exclusive.", "For a range [min, max], test min−1, min, min+1, max−1, max, max+1 when meaningful.", "Check empty, zero, maximum length, and overflow boundaries for non-numeric fields too."], "Quantity must be 1–10 inclusive.\nProbe: 0, 1, 2, 9, 10, 11.\nExpected: only 1 through 10 are accepted.", "Boundary values are not substitutes for valid workflow and business-rule tests.", "For a maximum length of 20 characters, which boundary trio is useful?", ["19, 20, 21", "10, 11, 12", "0, 5, 10"], 0),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Pairwise and combinatorial testing", "Cover important combinations without testing every permutation", "Combinatorial testing selects combinations of factors such as browser, role, locale, and feature state. Pairwise testing aims for every pair of factor values to occur at least once, which can find interaction defects with fewer tests than a full Cartesian product. Higher-order risks may need stronger coverage.", ["List factors, valid values, constraints, and combinations that are impossible.", "Use pairwise coverage for broad interaction coverage, then add risk-based combinations.", "Do not assume pairwise coverage catches defects that require three-way or sequence interactions."], "Factors: browser {A,B,C}; role {guest,member};\nlocale {en,fr}; payment {card,wallet}\nFull combinations: 3 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 24\nA pairwise set covers each valid pair\nwith fewer cases; add high-risk triples.", "Use a tested combinatorial tool for larger matrices and retain the coverage model with results.", "What does pairwise testing aim to cover?", ["Every possible combination of all values", "Every pair of factor values at least once", "Only one value per factor"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "White-box testing and code coverage", "Use implementation structure to expose untested paths", "White-box techniques use internal structure to design or measure checks. Statement coverage measures executed statements; branch coverage measures decision outcomes; condition coverage examines boolean subconditions. Coverage highlights gaps but does not prove assertions are meaningful or requirements are correct.", ["Choose coverage targets in context and inspect uncovered high-risk branches.", "Use branch and condition coverage for decisions with multiple outcomes.", "Pair structural coverage with requirements, risk, data variation, and meaningful assertions."], "if account.active and has_permission:\n    allow_access()\nelse:\n    deny_access()\n\nTest both outcomes and each condition's\ntrue/false effect; assert the access result.\nCoverage alone cannot prove the rule is right.", "High line coverage can coexist with weak tests if results are not asserted.", "Which measure checks both outcomes of a decision?", ["Branch coverage", "Requirement count", "Code formatting"], 0),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Decision tables and state transitions", "Cover combinations and state-dependent behavior", "Decision tables represent combinations of conditions and the action expected for each rule. State-transition testing checks valid states, events, transitions, and rejected transitions. Both are useful when behavior depends on combinations, history, or previous actions.", ["List conditions and outcomes, then remove impossible or duplicate rules.", "Model states, triggering events, guards, resulting state, and visible output.", "Test valid transitions, forbidden transitions, retries, and state persistence."], "Account lock rule\nAttempts | Account active | Outcome\n1–4      | yes            | reject login\n5        | yes            | lock account\nany      | no             | remain locked\n\nAlso verify unlock behavior and audit event.", "Test rules and transitions explicitly; do not assume the happy path covers their interactions.", "Which technique best models a workflow with Draft, Submitted, and Approved states?", ["State-transition testing", "Equivalence partitioning only", "Pairwise browser testing"], 0),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Exploratory, session-based, and usability testing", "Learn through structured investigation", "Exploratory testing combines learning, test design, and execution. A charter sets a goal and boundaries; session-based testing time-boxes exploration and records a debrief. Usability testing observes representative users trying realistic tasks and is distinct from simply asking whether they like a screen.", ["Write a charter with a mission, area, risks, and time box.", "Record paths, test ideas, questions, evidence, and defects during the session.", "Debrief findings and update cases or requirements based on what was learned."], "Charter: Investigate cart recovery after\nnetwork interruption (45 min).\nDebrief: 3 risks explored; 1 reproducible\nprice mismatch; attach video and network log.", "Exploration can find unknown risks, but evidence and follow-up are still needed.", "What makes a session-based exploration actionable?", ["A charter, notes, findings, and a debrief", "A pass/fail count with no context", "Random clicks without recording build or data"], 0),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Risk-based testing and prioritization", "Spend test effort where failure matters most", "Risk-based testing prioritizes tests using likelihood and impact of failure. Consider user harm, financial or privacy impact, frequency of use, technical complexity, change size, and detectability. Priorities should be revisited as evidence and scope change.", ["Identify risks at feature and system level; describe cause, failure, and consequence.", "Use a transparent likelihood/impact scale and prioritize mitigations, not just scores.", "Allocate deeper testing to high-risk changes and communicate lower-risk areas left uncovered."], "Risk = likelihood × impact (a prioritization aid)\nHigh: payment charged twice → P1 checks\nMedium: avatar crop differs on one browser\nLow: typo on an internal help page\nReassess after code and incident evidence.", "A risk score supports discussion; it is not a precise probability or a substitute for judgment.", "Which should usually receive earlier, deeper testing?", ["A low-impact color preference", "A changed payment authorization flow", "An unchanged footer link"], 1),
      lesson("Planning & test design", "Requirements review and traceability", "Turn requirements into observable coverage", "Testability review asks whether requirements are clear, consistent, feasible, and measurable. A traceability matrix links requirements to scenarios, cases, results, and defects. It helps expose gaps and change impact; counting links alone does not prove adequate coverage.", ["Ask for examples, constraints, error behavior, permissions, and acceptance thresholds.", "Link each requirement to planned tests and execution evidence.", "When a requirement changes, identify affected cases, data, automation, and risks."], "REQ-12: user may cancel before dispatch\n→ TS-21: cancel eligible order\n→ TC-21A: cancel before dispatch\n→ Run 1542: passed\n→ TC-21B: reject cancellation after dispatch", "Traceability shows what has and has not been checked; review whether the checks are meaningful.", "What can traceability most directly help identify?", ["Tests with no meaningful requirement or risk link", "All possible product defects", "Which code has the prettiest style"], 0),
      lesson("Test cases & test data", "Test scenario, test case, and test script", "Move from intent to repeatable evidence", "A test scenario describes a feature or behavior to investigate. A test case gives specific preconditions, data, steps, and expected results. A test script is an executable implementation, often automated. Cases should be understandable, independent where practical, and maintainable when requirements change.", ["Give cases a unique ID, objective, preconditions, data, steps, expected result, priority, and status.", "Write one clear action per step and a verifiable expected result for it or for the final outcome.", "Avoid ambiguous words like “works correctly”; name the observable screen, record, message, or event."], "TC-LOGIN-004 | Invalid password\nPrecondition: active user exists\nData: correct email, wrong password\nSteps: open sign-in; submit credentials\nExpected: generic error; no session;\nfailed attempt is recorded.", "A test case is a specification of a check, not just a title or a list of clicks.", "Which detail makes an expected result testable?", ["“The page looks good”", "“No session is created and the defined error is shown”", "“Check the login”"], 1),
      lesson("Test cases & test data", "Write cases from scenarios and user stories", "Derive happy paths, alternatives, and risks", "Start with the actor, goal, trigger, preconditions, and business rules in a scenario or user story. Derive successful, invalid, boundary, permission, duplicate, recovery, and relevant quality checks. Keep cases focused so a failure points to a specific behavior.", ["Clarify the story's acceptance criteria and any missing rules before inventing expected behavior.", "Cover primary flow, alternate flow, invalid inputs, boundaries, roles, state changes, and recovery.", "Link cases to the requirement and make each expected result observable and unambiguous."], "Scenario: Customer applies a gift card at checkout.\nCases: valid balance; zero balance; expired;\nunknown code; balance equals total; balance\nless than total; repeated submit; guest/user role;\nnetwork interruption; order and balance persistence.", "Use the scenario-based test case generator in Study resources to create an editable first-draft suite.", "What should be confirmed before writing expected results?", ["Acceptance criteria and business rules", "The test author's preferred wording", "The number of cases in another project"], 0),
      lesson("Test cases & test data", "Test data: design, privacy, and lifecycle", "Prepare realistic data safely and repeatably", "Test data is the input and system state needed to execute cases. It should cover relevant partitions, boundaries, roles, and histories without exposing real personal data. Data must be controlled, reproducible, isolated where possible, and cleaned or refreshed responsibly.", ["Use synthetic or properly masked data; never copy production personal data without approved safeguards.", "Record data ownership, setup, dependencies, reset, and expected state.", "Include valid, invalid, boundary, duplicate, expired, and role-specific records where relevant."], "Data set: customer-active-valid\nEmail: qa+active@example.test\nPlan: monthly; status: active\n\nCustomer-expired-card\nUse a sandbox token, not a real PAN.\nReset records before each independent run.", "A realistic-looking value is not automatically safe: remove identifying and secret data.", "What is a safe default for payment testing?", ["Use an approved sandbox token and synthetic data", "Reuse a real customer card number", "Store credentials in a committed test file"], 0),
      lesson("Test cases & test data", "Test environments and configuration", "Make results reproducible across systems", "A test environment combines software versions, services, configuration, infrastructure, devices, and network conditions. Environment differences can cause false failures or hide product defects. Record what was tested and keep secrets and personal data out of logs.", ["Track build, configuration, dependent-service versions, device, browser, and feature flags.", "Use isolated or controlled dependencies and make environment readiness observable.", "Separate environment failure from product failure with health checks and diagnostic evidence."], "Run record:\nBuild: 2.8.14+e91c\nBrowser: Chromium 132 / Windows 11\nAPI: staging v4; feature flag checkout_v2=on\nData set: synthetic-orders-v3\nRegion: eu-west test cluster", "Environment parity matters, but production-like should never mean uncontrolled production data or side effects.", "Why record the build and configuration with a test result?", ["To reproduce and interpret the evidence", "To guarantee the next build behaves the same", "To replace test data"], 0),
      lesson("Execution & defect management", "Test execution and result reporting", "Run cases consistently and preserve evidence", "Execution compares observed behavior with the expected result under recorded conditions. Use clear statuses such as pass, fail, blocked, and not run. A failure needs triage and evidence; a blocked case needs the dependency or environment issue documented.", ["Start only when entry criteria and environment/data are ready; record deviations.", "Capture actual results, build, data identifiers, logs, and screenshots or traces when useful.", "Summarize executed, passed, failed, blocked, not-run, coverage, and unresolved risk."], "TC-LOGIN-004 | FAIL\nBuild: 2.8.14 | Browser: Chromium\nExpected: no session after invalid password\nActual: session cookie issued\nEvidence: trace-1542.zip\nDefect: BUG-381", "Never mark a blocked or unexecuted test as passed to improve a report.", "What should a failed test result contain?", ["Only a red status", "Build, data, actual/expected behavior, and useful evidence", "An assumption about which developer caused it"], 1),
      lesson("Execution & defect management", "Defect lifecycle and workflow", "Track an issue from discovery to verified closure", "A defect workflow commonly includes new, triaged, assigned, in progress, fixed, ready for retest, verified, and closed. Teams may use different labels. Reopen a defect when the fix does not resolve the original problem; do not close it solely because code was merged.", ["Agree states, owners, service-level expectations, and transitions with the team.", "Retest the original steps and add regression checks around the changed behavior.", "Close with verification evidence, or reopen with current build, result, and reproduction steps."], "New → Triage → Assigned → In progress\n→ Ready for retest → Verified → Closed\n\nIf the same failure remains after retest:\nreopen with updated evidence.", "A status is useful only when people share what it means and what evidence permits a transition.", "When should a fixed defect be closed?", ["After code merge without validation", "After the fix is verified against the failure and relevant regression checks", "When the sprint ends"], 1),
      lesson("Execution & defect management", "Write high-quality defect reports", "Make failures fast to reproduce and assess", "A defect report communicates a concise title, environment, preconditions, exact steps, expected and actual results, reproducibility, impact, and evidence. Include only necessary sensitive data. A good report is neutral and separates observation from suspected cause.", ["Use a specific title such as “Checkout creates two orders after one submit.”", "List numbered steps and deterministic test data; say whether the issue is reproducible.", "Attach a screenshot, video, trace, logs, request ID, or sanitized payload when it helps."], "Title: One submit creates two paid orders\nBuild / environment: ...\nPreconditions / data: ...\nSteps: 1... 2... 3...\nExpected: one order and one charge\nActual: two orders and two charges\nReproducibility / impact / evidence: ...", "Report observable behavior, not blame or an unverified root cause.", "Which title is most useful?", ["“It is broken again”", "“Sometimes payment fails”", "“One checkout submit creates two paid orders”"], 2),
      lesson("Execution & defect management", "Severity, priority, and triage", "Describe impact separately from fix urgency", "Severity describes the technical or user impact of a defect; priority describes how urgently the organization should address it. A severe issue may be rare, and a visually minor issue may become urgent before a major event. Triage considers evidence, reach, workarounds, risk, and release context.", ["Use the team's agreed scale and describe impact in user/business terms.", "Set priority with product and engineering context; avoid equating severity and priority.", "Reassess when scope, usage, workaround, or release timing changes."], "Severity: high — unauthorized user can view another account.\nPriority: urgent — exposed in the current release.\n\nA cosmetic typo on a blocked internal page\nmay have low severity and low priority.", "Severity/priority labels are team conventions; justify them with impact and urgency.", "Which statement is correct?", ["Severity and priority always mean the same thing", "Severity describes impact; priority describes urgency to address", "Only developers can decide user impact"], 1),
      lesson("Execution & defect management", "Coverage, metrics, and test closure", "Report decision-useful evidence, not vanity numbers", "Useful reporting combines coverage and outcomes with risk and context. Examples include requirement coverage, execution status, defect trends, escaped defects, and time to feedback. Metrics can be gamed or misleading when treated as targets detached from quality.", ["State the denominator and scope behind percentages and counts.", "Pair pass rate with coverage, blocked/not-run tests, defects, and residual risk.", "At closure, state what was tested, what was not, known issues, and release recommendation evidence."], "Release summary:\nRequirements covered: 42 / 48 (6 deferred)\nCases: 130 passed, 4 failed, 3 blocked\nOpen: 1 high-impact payment issue\nRisk: Safari payment recovery unverified\nRecommendation: hold until blocker resolved", "A high pass rate does not imply low risk if critical areas were not tested.", "Which report is most honest?", ["100% pass rate without mentioning unrun cases", "Pass rate plus scope, blocked cases, open risks, and exclusions", "Defect count without build or release context"], 1),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Automation strategy and the test pyramid", "Put checks at the cheapest reliable level", "A test pyramid is a heuristic: many fast component checks, fewer service/API integration checks, and a smaller number of end-to-end UI checks. The right balance depends on risk and architecture. Automation strategy should specify what to automate, at which layer, and how results are maintained.", ["Prefer focused component checks for deterministic business rules.", "Test service contracts and integration behavior through APIs where useful.", "Reserve UI end-to-end automation for critical user journeys and real browser-specific behavior."], "Test distribution example:\nMany: unit/component checks\nSome: API, contract, integration checks\nFew: critical cross-system browser journeys\n\nUse risk and feedback time to tune the mix.", "The pyramid is a guideline, not a quota; avoid brittle UI tests for every small rule.", "Why keep the UI end-to-end suite focused?", ["UI checks are often slower and more sensitive to integration and timing", "UI tests cannot check user workflows", "Only UI tests can assert business rules"], 0),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Automate UI and API checks", "Choose the most direct reliable interface", "UI checks validate behavior through the browser and are valuable for critical user workflows. API checks validate service contracts, rules, and error handling with less interface overhead. Both need assertions on meaningful outcomes, deterministic data, and isolated state.", ["Use UI for user-visible flows, rendering, accessibility interactions, and browser-specific behavior.", "Use API for response schemas, authorization, validation, business rules, and service integration.", "Avoid asserting implementation details that users and dependent systems do not rely on."], "API: POST /orders with valid fixture\nAssert: 201, order ID, one persisted order\n\nUI: open checkout and submit order\nAssert: confirmation names same order ID\nand no duplicate submit is possible", "Use the lowest layer that provides trustworthy evidence for the risk being tested.", "Where is response-schema validation usually most direct?", ["API test", "Visual screenshot comparison only", "Manual release note"], 0),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Automation framework and maintainable design", "Build reusable checks without hiding intent", "An automation framework provides conventions for test discovery, fixtures, assertions, reporting, configuration, and integrations. Reuse should reduce duplication while keeping each test's intent visible. Page objects and helpers are useful when they improve readability, not as mandatory layers.", ["Define naming, tags, fixture lifecycle, assertions, logging, and failure artifacts.", "Keep test data and environment configuration outside test logic where appropriate.", "Review abstractions for clarity; avoid giant helpers that conceal actions and expected outcomes."], "test('member can view own order', async ({ page }) => {\n  await signInAs(page, memberFixture);\n  await page.goto('/orders/ORD-42');\n  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', {\n    name: 'Order ORD-42'\n  })).toBeVisible();\n});", "A readable test tells the next maintainer what behavior is protected and why.", "What is the goal of a shared test helper?", ["Hide every action behind an opaque API", "Reduce duplication while preserving scenario intent", "Avoid assertions"], 1),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Reliable locators, waits, and assertions", "Synchronize on observable conditions", "Reliable browser automation uses locators tied to accessible roles, labels, or stable test contracts. Fixed sleeps make tests slow and flaky. Wait for a meaningful condition, then assert the user-visible state and important side effects.", ["Prefer role/label/text locators that reflect the interface contract.", "Wait for a specific element, response, or state; do not guess timing with arbitrary delays.", "Assert outcomes, not only that a click or request completed."], "await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save' }).click();\nawait expect(page.getByRole('status'))\n  .toHaveText('Changes saved');\n\n// Avoid: waitForTimeout(3000)", "An action completing does not prove the intended business outcome occurred.", "Which synchronization approach is more reliable?", ["Always sleep for five seconds", "Wait for a specific visible saved state", "Retry the test until it passes"], 1),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Fixtures, setup, cleanup, and isolation", "Make each automated test independent", "Fixtures provide controlled prerequisites such as a browser, user, database record, or service stub. Tests should avoid order dependence and clean up created state. Isolation reduces cross-test interference and simplifies parallel execution and failure diagnosis.", ["Create unique records or reset data per test; do not depend on execution order.", "Use setup and teardown hooks or fixtures with explicit lifetime and ownership.", "Keep external side effects in sandboxes or stubs and ensure cleanup runs after failure."], "test.beforeEach(async ({ request }) => {\n  await resetTestAccount('qa-user-7');\n});\n\ntest('creates an order', async ({ page }) => {\n  // isolated state and deterministic data\n});\n\ntest.afterEach(async ({ request }) => {\n  await deleteOrdersFor('qa-user-7');\n});", "Isolation makes parallel runs safer and makes a failed test easier to reproduce.", "What is a sign that tests are not independent?", ["They pass when run alone but fail depending on suite order", "They use unique test IDs", "They clean up created records"], 0),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Flaky tests and debugging", "Find causes instead of hiding intermittent failure", "A flaky test produces different results without a relevant product change. Common causes include timing assumptions, shared state, unstable dependencies, race conditions, weak locators, and environment limits. Retries can gather evidence but should not become a permanent substitute for fixing causes.", ["Track failure frequency, run context, traces, logs, and recent environmental changes.", "Reproduce under controlled conditions and isolate data, timing, and dependency variables.", "Quarantine only with an owner, expiry, impact assessment, and a repair plan."], "Failure triage:\n1. Compare trace and logs for pass/fail runs.\n2. Check shared account and parallel workers.\n3. Replace fixed wait with observable condition.\n4. Re-run repeatedly; retain failure evidence.", "A retry that passes can mask a reliability issue; preserve and investigate first-failure evidence.", "What should happen to a quarantined flaky test?", ["It stays disabled without an owner", "It gets an owner, expiry, and fix plan", "It is counted as a pass"], 1),
      lesson("Automation foundations", "Continuous integration and quality gates", "Deliver fast trustworthy feedback on every change", "CI runs relevant checks automatically on changes and reports results with build context. Quality gates should be risk-based and actionable. They can include unit/API/UI checks, static analysis, security scans, and deployment smoke checks, with explicit policies for failures and flaky tests.", ["Run fast, high-signal checks early; schedule broader suites where appropriate.", "Publish logs, traces, reports, and test environment versions with each run.", "Block releases on agreed critical failures and provide an accountable exception process."], "Pull request gate:\n1. Lint and component tests\n2. API contract and integration suite\n3. Critical browser smoke tests\n4. Publish traces and reports\n5. Deploy only when required gates pass", "A quality gate without actionable diagnostics slows delivery without improving decisions.", "Which is a strong CI quality gate?", ["A critical failure blocks promotion and includes useful diagnostics", "A test failure is silently ignored", "Only a manually updated spreadsheet"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Regression, retesting, smoke, and sanity", "Know which test activity answers which question", "Retesting confirms that a specific reported defect has been fixed. Regression testing checks whether changes broke existing behavior. Smoke testing is a broad, shallow check that a build's critical paths are stable enough for further testing; sanity checks are narrow checks around a change. Team usage of these terms can vary.", ["Retest the original defect conditions on the candidate build.", "Choose regression checks based on changed components, dependencies, and risk.", "Run smoke checks after deployment or build delivery; expand testing only when they pass."], "Fix: order total rounding\nRetest: original rounding defect case\nRegression: discounts, tax, refunds, invoices\nSmoke: sign in, browse, add item, checkout\nSanity: targeted tax calculation changes", "Document local terminology because teams sometimes use smoke and sanity differently.", "Which check verifies the original fixed defect?", ["Retesting", "Smoke testing only", "Exploratory charter"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "API and integration testing", "Validate contracts, errors, and service boundaries", "API testing checks requests and responses, schemas, status codes, authentication, authorization, validation, and side effects. Integration testing checks interactions between services or components, including timeouts, retries, and failure handling. Test both expected success and meaningful errors.", ["Use explicit test data and assert status, headers, schema, and important persisted effects.", "Cover missing, malformed, unauthorized, forbidden, duplicate, and boundary requests.", "Stub unreliable external dependencies where appropriate and test contract compatibility."], "POST /v1/orders\nValid body → 201 + order ID\nMissing item → 400 + field error\nNo token → 401\nWrong account → 403\nRepeat idempotency key → one order", "A 200 response alone does not prove correct data, authorization, or side effects.", "Which response most commonly means authentication is missing or invalid?", ["401", "404", "503"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Database and data integrity testing", "Check persistence, constraints, and consistency", "Database testing verifies that application operations preserve data integrity, transactions, constraints, relationships, and expected query behavior. Validate through supported interfaces where possible, and use safe isolated databases rather than querying production data casually.", ["Check created, updated, deleted, and related records after user-visible operations.", "Test constraints, rollback behavior, duplicate prevention, and transaction consistency.", "Use migrations and representative synthetic volumes to check upgrade and performance risks."], "Create order with two lines.\nAssert order and both lines persist with\nmatching totals and foreign keys.\nForce payment failure.\nAssert transaction rollback leaves no paid order.", "Prefer stable observable contracts over coupling every test to internal schema details.", "What should a failed transaction test verify?", ["No partial state violates the product's consistency rules", "The database keeps half the order", "The UI hides the failure"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Accessibility and compatibility testing", "Verify inclusive use across assistive tech and platforms", "Accessibility testing checks whether people with disabilities can perceive, understand, navigate, and operate the product. Automated scans can catch some issues, but keyboard and assistive-technology evaluation is also needed. Compatibility testing covers agreed browsers, devices, operating systems, and configurations.", ["Check semantic headings, labels, names, focus order, visible focus, contrast, and error announcements.", "Test keyboard-only flows and representative screen-reader combinations for critical journeys.", "Select browser/device coverage from user analytics, support commitments, and risk."], "Manual checks:\nTab through form; verify visible focus\nSubmit invalid values; locate announced errors\nUse screen reader for labels and status\nRepeat on supported browser/device matrix", "Automated accessibility scans are useful but cannot establish full accessibility conformance.", "What is a necessary complement to automated accessibility scanning?", ["Keyboard and assistive-technology testing", "Removing form labels", "Testing only with a mouse"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Performance, load, and reliability testing", "Measure behavior under defined conditions", "Performance testing evaluates response time, throughput, resource use, and stability. Load testing checks expected traffic; stress testing explores beyond expected limits; endurance testing examines sustained behavior. Workload, data volume, environment, and service-level objectives must be defined for results to mean anything.", ["Set a representative workload, duration, data volume, and measurable objectives.", "Monitor latency percentiles, throughput, errors, CPU, memory, and dependencies.", "Distinguish capacity bottlenecks from test-generator or environment limits."], "SLO: search p95 < 400 ms at 300 users\nRun: 20 min ramp-up + 60 min steady load\nMeasure: p50/p95/p99, errors, throughput,\nCPU, memory, and database saturation", "An average response time can hide slow experiences; examine percentiles and error rates.", "Which metric best reveals the slowest 5% of requests?", ["p95 latency", "Mean only", "Number of test cases"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Security testing essentials", "Test trust boundaries and access rules safely", "Security testing evaluates whether controls protect confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Functional tests should verify authentication and authorization boundaries. Dedicated security assessments require suitable authorization, scope, and safeguards; do not test systems without permission.", ["Test unauthenticated and wrong-role access, direct object references, session handling, and safe error messages.", "Validate input handling and ensure sensitive values do not leak into logs or responses.", "Coordinate security testing on authorized environments and follow approved disclosure and escalation paths."], "User A requests own record → allowed\nUser A requests User B's record → denied\nNo session requests private route → denied\nLogs contain request ID, not credentials\nRun deeper scans only in authorized scope", "Never access real users' records or attack a system without explicit authorization.", "Which is an authorization check?", ["Verify a user cannot read another user's private record", "Check whether a password field has a label", "Measure page load time"], 0),
      lesson("Specialized testing & delivery", "Agile, DevOps, and continuous quality", "Collaborate on quality throughout delivery", "Agile teams refine requirements, deliver small increments, and review outcomes. DevOps extends shared ownership into deployment and operation. Continuous testing provides feedback throughout the pipeline, while production monitoring and incidents inform new tests. Neither means running every test on every change.", ["Refine acceptance criteria collaboratively before implementation and test as code evolves.", "Automate repeatable gates while retaining exploratory, accessibility, and risk-focused review.", "Feed production incidents and observability signals into test cases, monitoring, and design."], "Story ready: acceptance examples agreed\nDuring build: component and API checks\nBefore merge: risk-based UI and security checks\nAfter deploy: smoke + health monitoring\nIncident: add regression and observability", "Quality is a team responsibility; testing provides evidence and challenges assumptions.", "What is a useful outcome of a production incident review?", ["A regression test or monitoring improvement for the failure mode", "Removing the incident from reports", "Adding retries without investigating the cause"], 0)
    ]
  };

  const course = window.learningPaths["software-testing"];
  course.lessons.push(
    lesson("SDLC models", "SDLC models: compare and choose the right approach", "Understand how different models organize the same lifecycle", "An SDLC model describes how requirements, design, construction, testing, and release are organized. It does not remove lifecycle activities; it changes their order, feedback, and risk management. Common models include Waterfall, V-model, iterative/incremental, prototyping, Spiral, Agile, and DevOps/continuous delivery. Real teams often combine practices.", ["Waterfall: sequential phases; useful when scope is stable and formal approvals matter, but late feedback makes change costly.", "V-model: pairs each development stage with a corresponding test level; useful for traceability and regulated work.", "Iterative/incremental: deliver and learn in small slices; useful when needs evolve and early feedback matters.", "Prototyping: build a limited model to clarify user needs or usability; label throwaway prototypes so they are not mistaken for production-ready software.", "Spiral: repeat plan, identify/analyze risks, engineer, and evaluate; useful for large, uncertain, high-risk work but requires deliberate risk expertise.", "Agile: short collaborative iterations, a prioritized backlog, frequent review, and continuous testing; useful when teams can adapt scope and work closely with stakeholders.", "DevOps/continuous delivery: connect development and operations with automated integration, deployment, monitoring, and feedback; requires reliable automation and operational ownership."], "Example: A stable statutory report with fixed rules may suit a V-model with requirement-to-test traceability. A new shopping experience with uncertain customer behavior may use iterative delivery and prototypes. A high-risk aerospace change may use formal lifecycle gates plus risk analysis. A web service with frequent safe releases may use Agile delivery with DevOps automation and production monitoring.", "Choose a model for uncertainty, change cost, risk, compliance, feedback speed, and team capability—not because one model is universally best.", "Which statement about SDLC models is accurate?", ["Every model removes testing until the end", "Models organize lifecycle activities and feedback differently", "Agile means no planning or documentation"], 1),
    lesson("SDLC models", "Waterfall and V-model", "Compare sequential delivery with mapped verification", "Waterfall organizes work in sequential phases such as requirements, design, implementation, verification, and maintenance. It can provide clear approvals and traceability when needs are stable, but users may see working software late. The V-model makes verification planning explicit: each development artifact has a corresponding test level, with test design starting early.", ["Waterfall testing is often scheduled after implementation; mitigate late discovery with reviews, prototypes, and incremental evidence.", "The V-model pairs business requirements with acceptance testing, system design with system testing, architecture with integration testing, and component design with unit testing.", "Both models need change control, test data/environment planning, defect retest, and maintenance regression.", "Neither model guarantees quality; selection depends on uncertainty, feedback cost, compliance, and delivery constraints."], "Example: For a regulated records system, requirements are mapped to acceptance cases before build. Architecture decisions are linked to integration checks, component designs to unit checks, and release evidence is reviewed at formal gates. If a requirement changes, the team updates its trace links and affected tests before approving the change.", "In a V-model, test planning is not postponed until the code exists.", "Which V-model practice helps identify gaps early?", ["Plan corresponding tests while requirements and designs are being created", "Write every test only after release", "Skip integration evidence"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC models", "Iterative, incremental, and prototyping models", "Deliver slices and use feedback to refine understanding", "Iterative development revisits and improves a solution through repeated cycles. Incremental development delivers usable portions of capability over time. Prototyping creates a limited representation to learn about requirements, workflow, or usability. Teams may combine these approaches; each increment still needs acceptance criteria, integration, and regression checks.", ["An iteration improves or revises an existing solution; an increment adds usable capability.", "A prototype tests an assumption or communicates a design; decide whether it is disposable or will be hardened.", "Prioritize increments by user value, dependency, risk, and ability to obtain feedback.", "Test each increment and its interactions with previously delivered behavior."], "Example: A team first releases order search, then adds filters, then saved searches. A clickable prototype is reviewed with support agents before implementation. Each increment receives acceptance checks, integration/regression testing, and user feedback before the next slice is planned.", "A prototype is not automatically production quality; make the transition and risks explicit.", "What distinguishes an increment from an iteration?", ["An increment adds a usable piece; an iteration revisits and improves", "An increment is always untested", "An iteration is only a release meeting"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC models", "Spiral model and risk-driven development", "Repeat development cycles around explicit risk analysis", "The Spiral model organizes work in repeated loops that set objectives, identify and evaluate risks, develop and verify a solution, then review and plan the next loop. It is useful when uncertainty or consequences are high and risk reduction can guide what to build next. It has more planning overhead than a lightweight approach.", ["Set objectives, alternatives, constraints, and success criteria for each loop.", "Identify major risks—technical, safety, schedule, security, or usability—and choose evidence to reduce them.", "Engineer and verify the selected solution or prototype, then review results with stakeholders.", "Plan the next loop using remaining risk and evidence; stop, continue, or change direction deliberately."], "Example: Before committing to a new payment provider, the team runs an early loop to test authorization, latency, data handling, and failure recovery with a sandbox. The review finds timeout behavior is unclear, so the next loop resolves idempotency and recovery before full checkout implementation.", "Spiral is not simply repeating work; each loop should reduce a named risk or add justified capability.", "What primarily determines the next Spiral loop?", ["The most important remaining risks and evidence", "A fixed set of UI colors", "A desire to repeat the same tests without learning"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC models", "Agile delivery and the Scrum sprint", "Deliver small outcomes with continuous quality collaboration", "Agile is a set of values and principles for adaptive delivery, not one prescribed process. Scrum is a framework with accountabilities, events, artifacts, and a time-boxed sprint. Quality work belongs within each sprint: acceptance examples, design, implementation, testing, review, and improvement are shared responsibilities.", ["Keep a prioritized product backlog and refine upcoming items for clarity and testability.", "Set a sprint goal and select achievable work based on capacity, dependencies, and risk.", "Build and test continuously; meet the Definition of Done before calling a story complete.", "Review the product increment with stakeholders and use a retrospective to choose a process improvement."], "Example: The sprint goal is “members can safely reset a password.” Before planning, the team agrees link-expiry and single-use rules. During the sprint, developers and QA share test data and verify the happy, expired-link, and reused-link cases. At review, stakeholders try the flow; the retrospective assigns an owner to improve test inbox setup.", "A sprint is a delivery timebox, not a mini-waterfall with testing isolated at the end.", "Which practice best fits Agile quality?", ["Developers and testers collaborate and verify work throughout the sprint", "QA starts only after all sprint coding ends", "Acceptance criteria are removed to increase speed"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC models", "DevOps and continuous delivery lifecycle", "Connect build, release, operations, and learning", "DevOps is a collaborative approach that connects development and operations. Continuous integration frequently merges and validates changes. Continuous delivery keeps changes deployable with automated checks and controlled release decisions; continuous deployment automatically releases qualifying changes. Monitoring and incident learning feed the next development cycle.", ["Plan and code small changes with versioned configuration and ownership.", "Build and integrate continuously; run fast unit, contract, security, and risk-based end-to-end checks.", "Package once, promote the known artifact through environments, and use safe rollout/rollback controls.", "Monitor service and user outcomes; respond to incidents and add proportionate tests/observability."], "Example: A tested service build is promoted unchanged to staging, then released to a small user cohort. Smoke checks and error/latency dashboards are watched against agreed thresholds. If errors rise, the team rolls back; the incident review adds a regression case and a better alert before the next release.", "Deployment frequency without reliability and user outcome measures is not a complete quality goal.", "What connects the operations phase back to future development?", ["Monitoring and incident learning feed changes and tests into the next cycle", "Deleting production feedback", "Skipping release verification"], 0),
    lesson("Sprint planning & Jira", "Agile sprint lifecycle from refinement to retrospective", "Follow the work through one complete sprint", "A sprint is a time-boxed delivery cycle. The team refines backlog items before the sprint, agrees a goal and achievable work during planning, collaborates and inspects progress during the sprint, reviews the increment with stakeholders, then reflects and improves in the retrospective. Testing happens throughout the cycle, not only at the end.", ["Backlog refinement: clarify stories, acceptance examples, dependencies, risks, and rough estimates.", "Sprint planning: agree the sprint goal, select ready work based on capacity, and plan implementation and testing tasks.", "During sprint: build, review, test, integrate, update Jira, and collaborate daily to remove blockers.", "Sprint review: demonstrate the potentially releasable increment and gather feedback; retrospective: choose an improvement action."], "Example: In a two-week sprint, the goal is “members can reset passwords safely.” The team refines link expiry and duplicate-use rules before planning. Developers and testers work on the story and test cases together; the build is checked during the sprint. At review, the team demonstrates the completed flow; at retrospective, they agree to seed test inboxes earlier next sprint.", "A sprint review inspects the product; a retrospective inspects how the team worked.", "When should testing happen in a sprint?", ["Continuously as stories are developed and integrated", "Only after the sprint review", "Only when Jira says the sprint is complete"], 0),
    lesson("Sprint planning & Jira", "Sprint planning for QA and test work", "Plan quality tasks alongside feature work", "Sprint planning starts with a clear sprint goal and a prioritized, refined product backlog. The team checks capacity, availability, dependencies, and the Definition of Done before selecting work. For each story, include testing effort: requirement questions, case/data design, environment setup, execution, defect retest, and appropriate regression. Avoid treating testing as leftover time.", ["Before planning: review ready stories, acceptance criteria, risks, dependencies, and unfinished work.", "During planning: agree the goal, select work within actual team capacity, estimate collaboratively, and identify testing tasks/owners.", "Make the Definition of Done explicit: reviewed, integrated, tested at agreed levels, defects handled, and evidence recorded.", "Track blockers and capacity changes during the sprint; renegotiate scope instead of silently dropping verification."], "Example: A team has 8 available person-days after leave and support. A payment story is estimated at 3 days for implementation and 2 for testing/data/retest; an API story needs 2 days. The team discusses the remaining 1 day of capacity and risk before selecting additional work, rather than planning 10 days and hoping QA catches up.", "Estimates support a conversation; they are not individual performance targets or guarantees.", "What should happen when testing work does not fit the sprint's remaining capacity?", ["Renegotiate scope or capacity with the team and make risk visible", "Drop verification but mark the story done", "Hide the work from the sprint board"], 0),
    lesson("Sprint planning & Jira", "Jira defect lifecycle and workflow", "Track a defect from discovery through verified closure", "Jira defect workflows are configurable, so status names differ between teams. A common lifecycle is Open/New → Triaged → To Do/Selected → In Progress → Fixed/Resolved → Ready for QA → Retest → Closed. If the original issue still reproduces, reopen it with fresh evidence. Other valid outcomes include Duplicate, Not a Bug, Cannot Reproduce, Deferred, or Won't Fix; record the reason and link related work.", ["Create a Bug issue with a precise summary, affected version/environment, preconditions, numbered steps, expected/actual result, reproducibility, and sanitized evidence.", "Triage confirms validity, impact, severity, priority, component, owner, target release, and whether it blocks a sprint or release.", "The assignee fixes and links the change; QA verifies on the stated build, reruns the original steps, and adds risk-based regression.", "Close only after verification; reopen with updated build, steps, actual result, and evidence if it still fails."], "Example: QA finds that one checkout submit creates two charges. In Jira, create BUG-214 with the build, sandbox account, exact submit steps, expected one charge, actual two charges, and a sanitized trace. Triage marks it high impact and assigns the payment owner. The fix moves to Ready for QA; QA verifies one charge on the new build and checks retry behavior before closing. If two charges remain, reopen BUG-214 with the new trace.", "Agree your team's Jira status transitions and permissions; Jira does not impose one universal defect workflow.", "When should a Jira bug move to Closed?", ["After the fix is verified on the candidate build", "Immediately after it is assigned", "When its sprint ends, even if not retested"], 0),
    lesson("Sprint planning & Jira", "Jira bug fields, links, and board practices", "Keep defect tracking useful for the whole team", "A Jira issue is useful when it preserves enough context to reproduce, prioritize, and verify the problem. Teams commonly use project/issue type, summary, description, priority, severity (often a custom field), labels, component, affected/fix versions, assignee, sprint, and linked issues. Fields and workflows vary by Jira project. Avoid storing secrets, tokens, or real personal data in tickets.", ["Use a consistent Bug template: environment/build, preconditions, steps, expected/actual, frequency, impact, and sanitized attachments.", "Link the defect to its story, epic, related bugs, and fix when useful; set affects/fix versions accurately.", "Use board filters and dashboards to inspect new/untriaged, blocked, high-priority, sprint, and aging defects.", "Keep status, owner, priority, and retest evidence current; do not use labels as a substitute for agreed fields."], "Example Jira ticket: “BUG-214 — Checkout charges twice after a delayed submit.” Component: Payments. Affects version: 5.2. Priority: Highest. Sprint: Checkout reliability. Linked to story PAY-88. Evidence: sanitized trace and timestamps; no real card data. After the fix, record the verified build and link the regression case.", "Jira fields are often customized; follow the project's agreed template and required fields.", "What should never be attached to a Jira defect?", ["A real user's password or full payment credentials", "A sanitized reproduction trace", "The affected build version"], 0),
    lesson("Sprint planning & Jira", "Daily defect triage and sprint risk", "Use a short shared process to unblock quality work", "A defect triage reviews new and changed issues with product, engineering, and QA. The team validates evidence, removes duplicates, assesses impact and urgency, agrees ownership and next action, and decides whether sprint scope or release risk changes. Not every ticket needs a long meeting; clear preparation and decision ownership matter.", ["Review newly filed, reopened, aging, blocked, and release-critical defects.", "Agree valid/duplicate status, impact, priority, owner, target, dependencies, and verification plan.", "For sprint defects, communicate trade-offs and adjust the sprint backlog with the product owner.", "Record decisions and follow up on blocked items; never lower a priority solely to improve dashboard metrics."], "Example triage: A payment retry bug is reproducible only after a timeout and can double-charge a user. The group confirms impact and reproduction, assigns a payment owner, links the checkout story, adds a retry/idempotency verification case, and moves a lower-risk UI polish item out of scope to preserve capacity.", "Severity describes impact and priority describes urgency; triage should capture both rationale and owner.", "What is the outcome of useful triage?", ["A documented decision, owner, priority, and next action", "A ticket silently moved to Closed", "A larger number of Jira labels"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 1: discovery and requirements", "Define the problem, users, scope, and acceptance evidence", "The SDLC begins by understanding the business problem and users before committing to a solution. Product owners and analysts elicit requirements, explore constraints, identify stakeholders, and turn needs into testable functional and non-functional requirements. Early testing reviews expose ambiguity while changes are still inexpensive.", ["Inputs: business goals, user research, regulations, incidents, and existing-system constraints.", "Activities: stakeholder interviews, process mapping, story refinement, acceptance examples, dependency and risk analysis.", "Outputs: agreed scope, requirements or stories, acceptance criteria, assumptions, traceability links, and unresolved questions."], "Story: A member can reset a forgotten password.\nAcceptance questions: How long is the link valid?\nCan it be reused? What if the email is unknown?\nWhat happens to existing sessions?\n\nResolve these before expected results are fixed.", "A requirement is testable when its expected behavior and limits can be observed.", "When should testers review acceptance criteria?", ["Before implementation, while ambiguity can still be resolved", "Only after production release", "After deleting the original requirements"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 2: design and architecture", "Plan behavior, boundaries, data, and quality attributes", "Design turns approved requirements into user flows, interface behavior, data models, service contracts, and architecture. Quality attributes such as security, accessibility, reliability, and performance must be designed, not added as an afterthought. Reviews should identify failure modes and testability concerns before implementation.", ["Inputs: requirements, acceptance criteria, constraints, and risk analysis.", "Activities: UX and technical design, API/data contracts, threat modeling, testability review, and dependency planning.", "Outputs: reviewed designs, interface contracts, architecture decisions, non-functional targets, and updated risks/test approach."], "Design review checklist:\nCan errors be recovered from?\nAre authorization boundaries explicit?\nAre API errors and retry rules specified?\nCan important outcomes be observed?\nAre latency/accessibility targets measurable?", "A design review is a form of static testing: it can find issues before executable code exists.", "Which quality characteristic should be planned during design?", ["Authorization and threat boundaries", "Only the final test report", "A defect priority after release"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 3: implementation and code review", "Build small increments with developer-level quality checks", "During implementation, developers create code, configuration, migrations, and unit/component checks. Peer reviews and static analysis catch defects early. Continuous integration validates changes as they are committed. Testers collaborate on examples and risk while avoiding the assumption that developer tests replace independent system-level validation.", ["Inputs: reviewed design, acceptance examples, coding standards, and ready dependencies.", "Activities: implementation, code review, unit/component checks, static analysis, and integration into the main branch.", "Outputs: reviewable change, automated unit evidence, build artifacts, updated documentation, and resolved review feedback."], "Change pipeline:\nImplement a small requirement slice\n→ review code and tests\n→ run lint/static checks\n→ run component tests\n→ build artifact\n→ publish evidence for the next level", "Test code and product code both need review, meaningful assertions, and maintenance.", "Which activity can detect unreachable or suspicious code before execution?", ["Static analysis", "Production smoke test only", "Manual defect closure"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 4: integration, verification, and validation", "Check components together and confirm real user needs", "As components are integrated, teams verify contracts and interactions, then test the complete system against requirements. Acceptance activities validate whether the delivered product supports the intended business workflow. Defect triage and risk-based regression provide feedback for the next build.", ["Inputs: integrated build, requirements, test strategy, prepared environment, and controlled data.", "Activities: integration/API/system/non-functional testing, acceptance evaluation, defect triage, retest, and regression.", "Outputs: execution results, coverage and defect evidence, acceptance feedback, release risks, and candidate fixes."], "Verification: Does the order service enforce\nthe documented refund rule?\nValidation: Can support staff actually\ncomplete a customer refund workflow?\nBoth need explicit evidence and a known build.", "Verification against a specification and validation against user need are related but different questions.", "Which question is validation?", ["Does this build satisfy the defined schema?", "Does this workflow solve the user's real task?", "Did the linter finish successfully?"], 1),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 5: release and deployment", "Release a known artifact with safe deployment checks", "Release work packages a tested build, prepares configuration and data migrations, communicates changes, and deploys using controlled procedures. Smoke tests and health checks verify the deployment. Rollback or forward-fix plans reduce risk if important signals degrade.", ["Inputs: approved candidate, quality evidence, release notes, deployment plan, and rollback criteria.", "Activities: environment/configuration verification, staged rollout, migration checks, production smoke tests, and monitoring.", "Outputs: deployed version, release record, smoke evidence, known limitations, and monitored rollback/forward-fix decision."], "Release gate:\nConfirm artifact hash and configuration\nDeploy to a small cohort\nRun critical authorized smoke checks\nWatch error/latency/business signals\nExpand or rollback using defined thresholds", "Deployment is not complete when a pipeline says “success”; verify the deployed behavior and service health.", "What should a rollback decision use?", ["Predefined user-impact and service-health signals", "A guess based only on deployment duration", "The number of test cases written"], 0),
    lesson("SDLC phase walkthrough", "SDLC phase 6: operations and maintenance", "Use live evidence to improve reliability and future tests", "After release, teams monitor service-level indicators, support reports, incidents, security updates, and user feedback. Maintenance may fix defects, adapt the product, or prevent future failures. Incident learning should lead to proportionate code, test, monitoring, and process changes.", ["Inputs: live telemetry, incidents, customer feedback, vulnerability notices, and changing dependencies.", "Activities: monitor, triage, restore service, analyze causes, patch, and run targeted regression.", "Outputs: stable service, incident review actions, updated risks and tests, and refreshed support/runbook information."], "Incident loop:\nDetect → mitigate → restore\n→ review evidence and contributing factors\n→ add regression coverage and monitoring\n→ verify the repair in a safe environment", "A monitoring alert and a regression test serve different roles; use both when useful.", "What is a useful maintenance output after a confirmed software defect?", ["A regression check for the failure mode", "Deleting the incident history", "A guarantee that no more defects exist"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 1: requirement analysis and testability", "Find what is unclear, risky, and not yet verifiable", "Testing begins by reviewing requirements, stories, designs, and change impact. Testers identify test conditions, dependencies, data needs, risks, and unanswered questions. Testability analysis ensures an observable oracle exists: the team needs a way to determine whether the result is correct.", ["Inputs: requirements, designs, acceptance criteria, product risks, and change requests.", "Activities: review for ambiguity, consistency, feasibility, completeness, observability, and test-data/environment needs.", "Outputs: clarified acceptance criteria, test conditions, traceability candidates, risk register, and analysis questions."], "Requirement: “Search should be fast.”\nAnalysis question: What percentile, workload,\ndata volume, and threshold define fast?\nTestable form: p95 < 400 ms under the\nagreed workload and environment.", "A test without a known expected result is an investigation, not a reliable pass/fail check.", "What does testability analysis identify?", ["Whether expected behavior can be observed and judged", "The final count of defects in production", "The color of the test report"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 2: test planning", "Agree scope, approach, resources, and control points", "Planning decides what testing is needed, how it will be performed, who owns it, and what evidence is required. The plan adapts to risk, delivery model, constraints, and uncertainty. Entry and exit criteria make readiness and release decisions explicit without treating a checklist as a guarantee.", ["Inputs: analyzed requirements, product risks, team capacity, dependencies, and organizational policy.", "Activities: define scope/exclusions, levels/types, approach, estimates, roles, environments, data, schedule, and reporting.", "Outputs: reviewed test plan/strategy, estimates and assumptions, risk responses, entry/exit criteria, and communication plan."], "Entry: candidate build deployed; P1 environment\nhealth checks pass; synthetic data seeded.\nExit: critical scenarios pass; no open blocker;\nremaining risks and exclusions reviewed.\n\nDocument the agreed thresholds and owners.", "Entry/exit criteria must reflect release risk and should be revised when context changes.", "Which is a planning decision?", ["Which risk-based checks block release", "Whether the actual test passed", "Which unverified defect is already fixed"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 3: test analysis and design", "Derive scenarios, cases, and coverage from test conditions", "Analysis identifies what needs checking; design specifies how to check it. Select techniques that fit the requirement and risk, derive scenarios and cases, map data and expected results, and link coverage to requirements. Review cases with product and engineering to confirm business rules.", ["Inputs: test conditions, acceptance criteria, risks, and chosen test approach.", "Activities: select design techniques, create scenarios/cases, define expected results, prioritize, and review traceability.", "Outputs: reviewed test cases, coverage mapping, test charters, automation candidates, and identified data requirements."], "Condition: customer may cancel an order\nbefore dispatch.\nCases: eligible order; already dispatched;\nwrong customer; repeated cancellation;\nservice unavailable; status and refund recorded.\nTrace each case back to the rule.", "Choose cases for behavior and risk, not just to increase a case count.", "What should connect a test case to its test basis?", ["A requirement, acceptance rule, or identified risk", "A developer's personal preference", "A random production user's private data"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 4: test implementation and procedures", "Turn designed cases into executable, ordered checks", "Implementation prepares test procedures, suites, scripts, reusable fixtures, and execution order. Dependencies and priorities determine sequencing. Automation is developed where valuable; manual procedures remain clear and repeatable. Review scripts and cases before execution to catch setup gaps and ambiguous steps.", ["Inputs: reviewed cases, framework/tooling, data definitions, and environment plan.", "Activities: group cases, create scripts, prepare fixtures, establish execution order, and dry-run critical setup.", "Outputs: ready test suites/procedures, traceable automated checks, case identifiers, and known setup dependencies."], "Suite order:\n1. Environment health and authentication\n2. Component/API prerequisites\n3. Critical end-to-end workflows\n4. Recovery and regression cases\n5. Record results and artifacts by case ID", "A dry run can uncover broken preconditions before a release window is spent.", "Why group cases into suites?", ["To organize execution by risk, level, and dependency", "To hide which cases were not run", "To avoid recording results"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 5: environment and test-data readiness", "Prepare controlled conditions before running the suite", "Environment setup provisions the correct build, configuration, dependent services, device/browser, and network profile. Data setup creates safe, known starting states. Readiness checks separate environment problems from product failures and ensure cleanup/reset methods are available.", ["Inputs: environment specification, configuration, test procedures, and approved data policy.", "Activities: deploy the candidate, verify service health, seed/reset synthetic data, confirm access, and record versions.", "Outputs: readiness evidence, build/configuration record, safe data identifiers, known limitations, and approved go/no-go."], "Readiness record:\nBuild and feature flags\nBrowser/device/service versions\nSynthetic account and data-set ID\nHealth checks and dependencies\nReset/cleanup procedure\nKnown environment differences", "Never make production-like testing an excuse to expose real personal data or perform unapproved side effects.", "What is the purpose of a readiness check?", ["Confirm the environment and data can support valid test evidence", "Prove every requirement works", "Replace defect triage"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 6: execution, monitoring, and control", "Track progress and adapt the plan using evidence", "Execution records actual outcomes against expected results. Monitoring compares progress and risk with the plan; control actions can reprioritize cases, add coverage, resolve blockers, or update estimates. Preserve the distinction between pass, fail, blocked, and not run.", ["Inputs: ready build/environment/data, approved cases, and entry criteria.", "Activities: execute, capture evidence, report status, triage failures, retest fixes, and adjust priorities based on risk.", "Outputs: timestamped results, execution/coverage metrics, defect records, updated forecasts, and release-risk communication."], "If a blocking dependency is down:\nmark affected tests blocked, not passed;\nrecord scope and start time;\ntriage the environment issue;\nresume execution after readiness is proven.", "Monitor coverage and unresolved risk, not only the percentage of cases passed.", "How should an unexecuted case caused by an unavailable environment be reported?", ["Blocked", "Passed", "Closed as fixed"], 0),
    lesson("STLC phase walkthrough", "STLC phase 7: defect handling and test closure", "Verify fixes and close with residual-risk evidence", "Defect management records, triages, prioritizes, assigns, fixes, and verifies issues. Test closure evaluates planned versus actual coverage, unresolved defects, exclusions, metrics, and lessons learned. Closure supports a release decision; it does not claim that all defects have been found.", ["Inputs: execution results, defects and retest evidence, plan/criteria, and release context.", "Activities: verify fixes, rerun impact-based regression, reconcile traceability, report remaining risk, and capture improvements.", "Outputs: closure/summary report, residual risks and known issues, release recommendation evidence, retained artifacts, and follow-up actions."], "Closure checks:\nWere critical risks exercised?\nWhich cases failed, blocked, or were not run?\nWhat defects remain and who accepted the risk?\nAre artifacts and data handled safely?\nWhat should improve next iteration?", "A release decision belongs to accountable stakeholders using clear evidence and risk.", "What belongs in test closure?", ["Unresolved risk, coverage, results, and improvement actions", "Only the number of test scripts", "A promise that the product is defect-free"], 0),
    lesson("Testing technique lab", "Choose a test design technique", "Match the technique to the rule and risk", "A test technique is a systematic way to derive tests from requirements, structure, or experience. Black-box techniques use specified behavior; white-box techniques use internal structure; experience-based techniques use tester knowledge. Combine techniques when risks differ, and keep an explicit link from each test to what it checks.", ["Use equivalence partitions and boundaries for ranges and input classes.", "Use decision tables for combinations of business conditions and state transitions for history-dependent behavior.", "Use statement/branch techniques for code structure; use exploratory and error-guessing techniques to probe unknown risks."], "Input range → equivalence + boundaries\nRule combinations → decision table\nWorkflow states → transition coverage\nCode decision → branch coverage\nUnknown usability risk → exploratory charter\n\nSelect by risk, not by habit.", "No single technique is sufficient for every requirement or risk.", "Which technique best fits multiple independent business conditions?", ["Decision table", "Boundary values only", "Statement coverage"], 0),
    lesson("Testing technique lab", "Static testing: reviews, walkthroughs, and analysis", "Find defects without executing the software", "Static testing evaluates requirements, designs, test cases, code, and other artifacts without running the program. Reviews range from informal peer review to structured inspections. Static analysis tools can identify style, type, security, or complexity concerns, but their findings require appropriate interpretation.", ["Prepare the artifact and review criteria; focus on a specific risk or question.", "Record defects and questions, distinguish findings from suggestions, and assign follow-up.", "Recheck corrections and combine review evidence with dynamic tests where behavior must be exercised."], "Review a reset requirement:\nIs link expiry defined?\nDoes a reused token have a rule?\nAre unknown accounts handled safely?\nCan the expected result be observed?\nLog issues with artifact location and evidence.", "A walkthrough is collaborative explanation; an inspection is more formal and uses defined roles/process.", "Which is static testing?", ["Reviewing a requirement for ambiguity before code runs", "Submitting a request to a running API", "Load testing a deployed service"], 0),
    lesson("Testing technique lab", "Use-case and end-to-end scenario testing", "Cover complete user goals and alternate paths", "Use-case testing derives tests from actor goals, preconditions, main success flow, alternate flows, and exceptional paths. End-to-end scenarios verify important behavior across integrated components. Keep cases focused enough that failures remain diagnosable, and avoid assuming every back-end detail needs a browser test.", ["Identify actor, trigger, preconditions, success path, alternate paths, and postconditions.", "Derive one or more cases for each meaningful alternate and exception.", "Assert visible outcomes and important persisted effects at the appropriate test level."], "Use case: buy an item\nMain flow: select → pay → confirmation\nAlternates: item unavailable; payment declined;\nuser cancels; duplicate submit; network retry.\nPostcondition: one order with correct status.", "An end-to-end test validates an integrated journey, but targeted lower-level tests localize failures faster.", "What is a useful source for alternate-flow test cases?", ["A use case's alternate and exception paths", "The CSS class names", "The number of lines in a test script"], 0),
    lesson("Testing technique lab", "Experience-based testing and error guessing", "Use risk knowledge to probe beyond scripted cases", "Experience-based techniques use tester knowledge, product history, checklists, and likely failure patterns. Error guessing targets plausible mistakes such as off-by-one values, stale sessions, duplicate clicks, timeouts, and inconsistent time zones. These tests complement systematic techniques rather than replacing them.", ["Review incidents, support tickets, code changes, architecture, and similar past failures.", "Turn guesses into explicit probes with controlled data and observable expected behavior.", "Record successful findings as reusable cases or automation when they protect a recurring risk."], "Error-guessing prompts:\nWhat if the user double-clicks?\nWhat if the session expires mid-save?\nWhat if the clock crosses midnight/UTC offset?\nWhat if retry follows a timeout after commit?\nWhat if two users update the same record?", "Use prior failures to improve coverage, but do not assume the same defect pattern is the only risk.", "Which is an experience-based probe?", ["Retry a timed-out save to check for duplicate records", "Choose min/max from a specified range", "Measure statement coverage"], 0)
  );
})();
